INT. LAKESIDE CABIN - PORCH - EVENING

A screened porch with four mismatched rocking chairs and a card table. A citronella candle burns in a coffee can. Crickets. The lake is the color of pewter.

GERALDINE "GERRY" MUKHERJEE, fifties, tends a pitcher of lemonade. Her brother RAYMOND MUKHERJEE, sixty, wears a fishing vest and no fish. Their old friend OPHELIA TANAKA-DUBOIS, fifty-five, knits something that might be a scarf. And OSCAR LINDQVIST, thirty, Gerry's nephew by marriage, sits on the steps with a laptop he's not using.

They've been there a long time. The pitcher is almost empty.

GERRY
So that's three years since anyone caught a fish at this lake.

RAYMOND
I caught one in July.

GERRY
That was a boot.

RAYMOND
It was shaped like a fish.

OPHELIA
It was a boot, Raymond.

RAYMOND
A fish-shaped boot.

OSCAR
Is there a Wi-Fi password?

GERRY
There's no Wi-Fi, Oscar.

OSCAR
At all?

RAYMOND
There's the lake.

OSCAR
That's not Wi-Fi.

RAYMOND
No, but it's what we've got.

Ophelia holds up her knitting. It is a lumpy tube.

OPHELIA
What does this look like?

GERRY
A sock.

RAYMOND
A scarf.

OSCAR
A tube.

OPHELIA
It's a tube. I wasn't sure.

GERRY
Then why did you knit it?

OPHELIA
I thought it would find its shape.

RAYMOND
They never do.

OPHELIA
Raymond, you can be very negative.

RAYMOND
I'm a realist.

OPHELIA
You're a pessimist with a fishing license.

RAYMOND
That's cruel.

OPHELIA
It's accurate.

GERRY
You two should marry.

RAYMOND
GERRY!

OPHELIA ^
Gerry!

They glare at her. She sips her lemonade, unrepentant. Oscar looks up from the laptop.

OSCAR
Wait, were you serious? Because I could use the extra drama.

GERRY
I was never serious in my life.

RAYMOND
That's not true.

GERRY
Name one time.

RAYMOND
Nineteen eighty-seven. You wanted to be a lion tamer.

GERRY
I was seventeen.

RAYMOND
You bought a whip.

GERRY
It was a very good whip.

OPHELIA
She used it on the neighbor's goat.

GERRY
It was trespassing.

OSCAR
This is better than the internet.

RAYMOND
It's called the family, Oscar.

OSCAR
I know.

RAYMOND
No. I don't think you do.

Raymond rises from his rocker, goes to the screen, and looks out at the lake.

RAYMOND (CONT'D)
When I was a boy, our father brought us here. We'd sit on this very porch and he'd say, "Listen." And we'd listen. And you know what we heard?

OSCAR
Crickets?

RAYMOND
Nothing. It was beautiful.

GERRY
He had a lot of theories about nothing.

OPHELIA
He had a lot of theories about everything.

GERRY
He was right about the goat.

OPHELIA
That is true.

RAYMOND
Let's all be quiet for a minute.

OSCAR
Can I keep typing?

RAYMOND
No.

OSCAR
Fine.

The four of them sit in silence. The lake laps. A loon calls from somewhere out of sight.

GERRY
(after a beat)
Is anyone going to catch a fish?

RAYMOND
No.

OPHELIA
No.

OSCAR
No.

GERRY
Good. Then we can finish the lemonade.
